{"title":"2013 USAMO Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2013_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be a positive integer.  There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/c/65c448713d12675007d0a39c52cdf4ea1d88f917.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tfrac{n(n+1)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"46\" height=\"23\" /> marks, each with a black side and a white side, arranged into an equilateral triangle, with the biggest row containing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> marks.  Initially, each mark has the black side up.  An <i>operation</i> is to choose a line parallel to the sides of the triangle, and flipping all the marks on that line.  A configuration is called <i>admissible</i> if it can be obtained from the initial configuration by performing a finite number of operations.  For each admissible configuration <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/b/e3b15a3f355abb45de20b0e064215a711dbf357a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> denote the smallest number of operations required to obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> from the initial configuration.  Find the maximum value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/b/e3b15a3f355abb45de20b0e064215a711dbf357a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> varies over all admissible configurations.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2013_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Solution by inconsistent \n</p><p><br />\nThis problem is headache-inducing... but also fun. Ouch.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/2/8821ef2c0dc22eedbd65b26dfbeb8397b70ac447.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 4k + b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"86\" height=\"16\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/d/cbd9850629db2e46c09cab275bf0d207f1e8f6d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b \\in {1, 2, 3, 4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"18\" />. Then I claim the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/b/e6b9cfb1bde099db0e09f5db499521686df86838.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6k+1, 6k+2, 6k+3, 6k+6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"228\" height=\"18\" /> in these four cases. First, note that the base cases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/6/c965e528400c435a16a7cbb17ccb0ee249930b73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 1, 2, 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"80\" height=\"17\" /> are trivial by checking to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/a/0fafafcb8dbe78fec70b78cd2a2f4a0222372f2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1, 2, 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" />. Now note the case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/1/5218af40079081e694d8b82eeac2d368ef6ea76a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" /> needs at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to construct a black triangle with a single white mark at its center by checking. Call this size <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> triangle the evil triangle.\n</p><p>To show the lower bound, notice we can take the top <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> rows of a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/a/46aba6908f07148ec36b64bdeb19704eba9fef40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> equilateral triangle to be the limiting case for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and put two evil triangles on the bottom left and bottom right, respectively: these force <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> new rows to be occupied since each evil subcase requires at least two rows in every direction, which is a claim easy verified.\n</p><p>To show the upper bound, we reparameterize the triangles in terms of their rows. Notably, we can start counting rows from each corner, and give each mark a barycentric coordinate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/0/d80414f2f94f70731e91b6e12c4e6934c8fa65de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a, b, c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"57\" height=\"20\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc6d9a6172d35127b6cb37ea92192caf7029a54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> are integers denoting the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />th row in a given direction the point lies in, and this satisfies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/c/a1c288abdf6e8703482002145a00aff868dc598f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b+c = 2n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"147\" height=\"16\" /> by Viviani's theorem.\n</p><p>Now, consider the configuration <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> that minimizes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/b/e3b15a3f355abb45de20b0e064215a711dbf357a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" />. We place the rows in boxes: odd rows of each direction, and even rows of each direction, to make six boxes total. Call the numbers of odd rows <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/acc6d9a6172d35127b6cb37ea92192caf7029a54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> and the even rows <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/5/575c4548e40366c21be433d070ba6a1cc5a5f1ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d, e, f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"47\" height=\"18\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/0/d80414f2f94f70731e91b6e12c4e6934c8fa65de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a, b, c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"57\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d2743819a4b0307dddb8322cdb33d5e6929bc780.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(d, e, f)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"61\" height=\"20\" /> correspond to the same directions respectively in that order. Notice no row is operated on twice since row operations are commutative. Assume for sake of contradiction our claim is wrong, then we can construct a solution for the case one higher than our claim.\n</p><p>We now perform casework: for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/b/efb996ca207df5d210cd86914aed764adb55fcb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" />, it follows one of the quantities <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/2/8e261f7c516aed4db87b89821bf84feb0028477c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b+d+e, b+c+e+f, c+a+f+d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"325\" height=\"18\" /> is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. In this case, invert the rows chosen in those four boxes (i.e. remove the ones chosen and choose the ones unchosen). This fixes the configuration and decreases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/b/e3b15a3f355abb45de20b0e064215a711dbf357a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" />, giving contradiction.\n</p><p>Now, consider the case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/1/851b03ce92c3ad8da97daf2785cf4e103c7a8d66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" />. Like the above paragraph, we know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/d/5adaa08a4c29a49f46db2bf74073366a6a24f11f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+d, b+e, c+f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"139\" height=\"18\" /> are equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/2/a02c1fcd5cffa34ee9d707befb92edce5690743d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2k, 2k+1, 2k+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"138\" height=\"18\" /> in some order since if any pair sums to more than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e72c7016c6369f44ebe1c8cef90078a2a8dd3bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4k+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" /> then we reach a contradiction. But then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/8/868ad86351fc65df9da51c264a8dabff26f638e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2k+1 + 2k + 1 &gt; 4k + 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"200\" height=\"16\" />, giving contradiction.\n</p><p>Now, for the two remaining cases, we show a lemma: the xor of all the odd rows in two directions and all the even row in a third direction, or the sum of all the even rows in all directions are both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. This result follows immediately from our earlier result via Vivani's theorem (it cannot be that all three claims are true, and it cannot be the case that two of the claims are false and one is true by parity).\n</p><p>Thus in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/7/197213da9d06b2c5b59a10ad17f6a4aeb43d6697.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" /> case it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/2/2f2c3a683ed6846d2690cd34db36003e2a4a8fb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\min(a+b+f, b+c+d, c+a+e, d+e+f) \\geq \\frac{12k+6}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"443\" height=\"39\" /> so one of the triples is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/9/cb9709ef64d827b77e8ea7cd3afe7a6f8881157f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3k + 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"52\" height=\"16\" />. However inverting the three involved boxes, that total to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/3/563fd9e732f1a56875fa7457251e325c4f8c3602.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6k+3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> rows and don't affect the configuration when all three are inverted, decreases the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/b/e3b15a3f355abb45de20b0e064215a711dbf357a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" />, giving contradiction.\n</p><p>Finally, in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/6/246ec286b9641f91f9dfa14932e47171e2fad854.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b = 3$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/8/21803e5b17996c21504951c5e0690acea308c046.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a+b+f)+(b+c+d) + (c+a+e) + (d+e+f) =12k+8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"495\" height=\"20\" />. In particular, the odd/odd/even triples consist of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/d/38d2989f3068bc2e11f23ae67cf8b3f1e32ba2ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n+1}{2} + \\frac{n+1}{2} + \\frac{n-1}{2} = 6k+5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"260\" height=\"38\" /> rows each, while the even/even/even triple consists of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/4/004df8dc37c35c28546034297730d9846b8ee33f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot \\frac{n-1}{2} = 6k+3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"145\" height=\"38\" /> rows. For each of the triples to avoid decreases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/b/e3b15a3f355abb45de20b0e064215a711dbf357a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> after being inverted, it needs to satisfy either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/7/777d020ccf68214a8542f1d7a9ca128ad1bcecb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\leq 3k + 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"71\" height=\"17\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/3/6236044cd5edb95f4b115a042a28604393fc3ea2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\leq 3k+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"71\" height=\"17\" /> depending on the two cases, so it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/7/6b7bc5ef18176f7cb8f82d523e88c3ec3d78ef31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$LHS \\leq 12k + 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"127\" height=\"17\" />, giving contradiction. \n</p><p>Thus we have reached contradiction in all cases of counterexamples, proving our desired claim.\n</p>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017233132\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.026 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.041 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 186/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 335/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 9505/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.092      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n100.00%    5.092      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:11944:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017233132 and revision id 240119. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}